    "description": "JavaScript arrays beyond the basics: map/filter/reduce, searching helpers (includes, indexOf, find, findIndex), the string-based default sort pitfall with proper numeric comparators, destructuring and spread, plus arrays of objects - the shape real data usually has.",
    "learningNotes": {
      "whatILearned": "map/filter/reduce never mutate the original array, which makes code easier to reason about. Chaining them (filter then map) reads almost like a sentence. Spread [...] is the cleanest copy/merge tool.",
      "challenges": "[100, 25, 7, 90].sort() returning [100, 25, 7, 90] in string order surprised me - numeric sorts always need (a, b) => a - b.",
      "nextSteps": "Reducing to objects (grouping/frequencies), and combining array methods with fetch + JSON from an API."
    }
